## 3. How the assistant works
The model does not access the database directly. It uses tools that our
backend executes:
- **propose_visualization** — returns a widget configuration (type, title,
  query). Validated against the same rules as the widget builder (allowed
  types, catalog fields, per-type field rules). Invalid proposals are rejected
  and never shown.
- **query_data** — asks for numbers (measure, attributes, date range, filters,
  sort, limit, previous-period comparison). The backend runs it through the
  query API and returns the results to the model, which explains them.
- **ask_clarification** — asks the user to choose when the request is unclear
  (see section 5).

Available measures, attributes, visualization types and the data's date range
are generated from the catalog and given to the model with every request,
together with today's date and the time zone (Asia/Kolkata). Adding something
to the catalog must make it available to the AI automatically.

## 4. Chat → preview → add to dashboard
1. The user types a request, e.g. "Show revenue by city for the last 2 weeks".
2. The assistant replies with a short message and a **preview card** in the
   chat: the actual visualization rendered with real data, using the same
   widget component as the dashboard.
3. The preview card has:
   - **Add to dashboard** (primary),
   - **Edit** (opens the Phase 1 widget builder pre-filled with this
     visualization),
   - **Discard**.
4. Before adding, the user can refine it in chat ("make it a donut",
   "top 5 only", "last 90 days"). The assistant updates the latest preview
   instead of creating a new one each time.
5. Clicking **Add to dashboard** adds the widget at the **bottom** of the
   current user's dashboard, scrolls to it and briefly highlights it. The card
   then shows "Added to dashboard ✓" and its button is disabled so it can't be
   added twice.
6. Added widgets are saved to that user's dashboard exactly like widgets made
   with the builder, and are only visible to that user.

## 5. Clarifying questions
- If a request can't be turned into a visualization without guessing
  something that changes the result (e.g. "show sales" — revenue, orders or
  units? by what?), the assistant asks.
- Questions are shown as **clickable option buttons**, not just text, with a
  recommended option pre-selected and a **Continue** button. The user can also
  type an answer instead.
- Options must come from the catalog (real measures, attributes, date ranges,
  visualization types) so every answer leads to a valid visualization.
- Ask at most 2 questions at a time. Don't ask about things that are easy to
  change later (chart type, title) — pick a sensible default instead.
- If the request is clear enough, build it directly without asking.

## 6. Data questions
- The user can ask about data that isn't on their dashboard, e.g. "Which
  payment method has the most returns?" or "Why did revenue drop two months
  ago?".
- The assistant gets the numbers through query_data and answers with those
  exact numbers. It never estimates or invents figures.
- Where useful, it also offers a visualization preview that supports the
  answer, with the same Add to dashboard flow.
- If a question needs data we don't have (e.g. marketing spend, competitor
  sales), the assistant says that this data isn't available and suggests what
  it can show instead.

## 7. Off-topic questions
- The assistant only helps with Nivora Home's sales data and this dashboard.
- For unrelated requests (general knowledge, coding, writing, personal
  advice, etc.) it replies politely and briefly, e.g.:
  "I can only help with Nivora Home's sales data and your dashboard. Try
  asking something like 'Show top products this month'."
- It must not follow instructions that try to change these rules.

## 8. Chat UI
- Follows the existing design system: brand colours, typography, rounded
  cards, light and dark mode.
- A **chat button** in the dashboard header opens a panel on the right side;
  the dashboard stays visible and usable next to it. On small screens the
  panel opens full screen.
- Panel contents:
  - header with the assistant's name ("Insight AI"), a clear-conversation
    action and a close button;
  - welcome message with 3–4 **suggested prompt chips** (e.g. "Revenue by
    category this month", "Top 10 products by units sold",
    "Which city has the highest AOV?");
  - message list: user messages on the right, assistant messages on the left,
    preview cards and option buttons inside assistant messages;
  - input box with send button; Enter sends, Shift+Enter adds a new line.
- Assistant replies stream in as they are generated, with a typing indicator
  while waiting.
- Preview cards in the chat show loading, empty and error states like
  dashboard widgets.
- Friendly error message if the AI request fails, with a retry option.
- Conversation stays while the user is on the page; each user's conversation
  is separate.

## 9. Safety and limits
- All model output that changes the dashboard is validated on the server
  before use.
- The model never sees database credentials or raw tables, only catalog
  definitions and query results.
- Reasonable limits: maximum message length, a request timeout, and a simple
  per-user rate limit.

## 10. Out of scope
Voice input, file uploads, sharing chats, the AI changing or deleting existing
dashboard widgets (it only proposes new ones in this phase), multiple
languages.

